    "text": "To determine the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n \\)-th roots of unity, we start by solving the equation \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\).\n\nFirst, let's make a substitution to simplify the equation. Let \\( w = z^2 \\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[\nw^2 + w + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\( w \\). We solve it using the quadratic formula \\( w = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 1 \\), and \\( c = 1 \\):\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solutions for \\( w \\) are:\n\\[\nw = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad w = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the values of \\( z \\) such that \\( z^2 = w \\). We consider both solutions for \\( w \\):\n\n1. For \\( w = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\):\n   \\[\n   z^2 = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n   \\]\n   This complex number can be written in polar form. The magnitude is:\n   \\[\n   \\left| \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right| = \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{-1}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{3}{4}} = \\sqrt{1} = 1\n   \\]\n   The argument (angle) is:\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{-1} \\right) = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, in polar form:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} = e^{i \\frac{2\\pi}{3}}\n   \\]\n   Solving \\( z^2 = e^{i \\frac{2\\pi}{3}} \\):\n   \\[\n   z = \\pm e^{i \\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad z = \\pm e^{i \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\pi \\right)} = \\pm e^{i \\frac{4\\pi}{3}}\n   \\]\n\n2. For \\( w = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\):\n   \\[\n   z^2 = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n   \\]\n   Similarly, the magnitude is 1 and the argument is:\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left( \\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{-1} \\right) = -\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, in polar form:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} = e^{-i \\frac{2\\pi}{3}}\n   \\]\n   Solving \\( z^2 = e^{-i \\frac{2\\pi}{3}} \\):\n   \\[\n   z = \\pm e^{-i \\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad z = \\pm e^{-i \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{3} + \\pi \\right)} = \\pm e^{-i \\frac{4\\pi}{3}}\n   \\]\n\nCombining all the solutions, we have:\n\\[\nz = e^{i \\frac{\\pi}{3}}, \\quad z = e^{i \\frac{4\\pi}{3}}, \\quad z = e^{-i \\frac{\\pi}{3}}, \\quad z = e^{-i \\frac{4\\pi}{3}}\n\\]\n\nThese roots are all \\( 6 \\)-th roots of unity because:\n\\[\n(e^{i \\frac{\\pi}{3}})^6 = e^{i 2\\pi} = 1, \\quad (e^{i \\frac{4\\pi}{3}})^6 = e^{i 8\\pi} = 1, \\quad (e^{-i \\frac{\\pi}{3}})^6 = e^{-i 2\\pi} = 1, \\quad (e^{-i \\frac{4\\pi}{3}})^6 = e^{-i 8\\pi} = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n \\)-th roots of unity is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
